sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch08-05> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The roughness and randomness of fracture paths does not come from random irregularities in a material but from a general mechanism in which deterministic interactions of element displacements around a moving crack intrinsically generate randomness, as in a simple cellular automaton model.";
    dc:type "model-of-nature";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Randomness of fracture surfaces comes from a material-independent intrinsic mechanism";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 8, “Implications for Everyday Systems”; section “The Breaking of Materials” (from p. 374), p. 375. Scope: fracture of solids, from single crystals to geological faults"
}
